{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Important findings:\n1. `plan --dry-run` outputs 0 `{{` (good, this already passes)\n2. `easy --dry-run -r \"\"` exits with code 0 and does NOT report \"requirement cannot be empty\" — it just runs the dry-run and prints the prompt.\n\nSo the test's expected behavior for `easy --dry-run -r \"\"` is \"requirement cannot be empty\", which currently does NOT happen. This is a RED test — the test should fail.\n\nWait, but let me think carefully. The task6 scope is about migrating orchestration to handler. Does task6 include changing the `easy --dry-run -r \"\"` behavior? Let me re-read the test method.\n\nThe test method 边界 section says:\n- 输入 = `plan --dry-run`、`easy --dry-run -r \"\"`\n- 操作 = `./bin/rick plan --dry-run | grep -c '{{'`（预期 0）+ `./bin/rick easy --dry-run -r \"\"`（预期报 `requirement cannot be empty`）\n\nSo the test expects `easy --dry-run -r \"\"` to report \"requirement cannot be empty\". This is an acceptance criterion. The implementation (task6) should make this happen.\n\nHmm wait, but this might be tricky. Let me think about whether the empty requirement check should apply to dry-run mode. The current `runEasyMode` has the empty check, but `runEasyDryRun` doesn't. The task says migrate `runEasyDryRun`→`handler.EasyDryRun`.\n\nActually, looking at it more carefully — the test says \"预期报 `requirement cannot be empty`\". So the test script should check that the output contains \"requirement cannot be empty\" when running `easy --dry-run -r \"\"`.\n\nCurrently it does NOT. So this test step would currently FAIL (RED). Good, that's expected for TDD.\n\nActually wait — I need to be careful about whether this is a genuine requirement of task6 or whether I'm misreading. Let me re-read:\n\n\"边界（dry-run 变量替换 + 空 requirement）：前置条件 = build 成功；输入 = `plan --dry-run`、`easy --dry-run -r \"\"`；操作 = `./bin/rick plan --dry-run | grep -c '{{'`（预期 0）+ `./bin/rick easy --dry-run -r \"\"`（预期报 `requirement cannot be empty`）；预期 = 无 `{{` 且空 requirement 报错。\"\n\nYes, this clearly states that `easy --dry-run -r \"\"` should report \"requirement cannot be empty\". This is part of the acceptance criteria.\n\nSo the test script needs to check this. Currently it fails (RED).\n\nNow let me verify the exception cases. Let me run them.\n\nFor `doing job_nonexistent`: current `executeDoingWorkflow` checks `if _, err := os.Stat(jobDir); os.IsNotExist(err) { return fmt.Errorf(\"job directory not found: %s\", jobDir) }`. So it should report \"job directory not found\" with a non-zero exit.\n\nFor `plan --job job_nonexistent`: current `reEnterPlanWorkflow` checks `if _, err := os.Stat(jobPlanDir); os.IsNotExist(err) { return fmt.Errorf(\"job %s plan directory does not exist, use 'rick plan' to create a new job\", existingJobID) }`. The message is \"job job_nonexistent plan directory does not exist, ...\". So it contains \"plan directory does not exist\".\n\nFor `easy --ctx <已有 loops 的路径>`: `validateCtxInheritance` returns \"local context already exists (%s). Remove it first or omit --ctx\". So \"local context already exists\".\n\nLet me verify these behaviors now with actual commands.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtlety. For `plan --job job_nonexistent`, the RunE first checks `if existingJobID := GetJobID(); existingJobID != \"\"`. The `--job` is a global persistent flag (in root.go), so `--job job_nonexistent` sets `jobID` global variable. Then `GetJobID()` returns \"job_nonexistent\". Then it goes to `reEnterPlanWorkflow(\"job_nonexistent\", requirement)` which checks the plan dir.\n\nActually wait — but there's also `--dry-run` check. With `plan --job job_nonexistent` (no dry-run), it goes to re-enter.\n\nLet me verify all these behaviors.\n\nFor `easy --ctx`, I need a path that has existing loops. The local `.rick` dir is `/workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/.rick`. Does it have loops/ with entries? Let me check.\n\nLet me run all the exception cases."}}
