{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"I now understand the conventions used in the existing test scripts:\n- `find_repo_root` walks up to find `.git`\n- `run` helper returns (returncode, stdout, stderr)\n- `tail` helper for truncating error messages\n- `read_text` helper\n- Build to a temp dir (not directly to bin/rick) to avoid polluting. Actually task5 builds to a temp dir. But the task6 test method says `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick`. Hmm.\n\nLet me think. The task6 test method explicitly says:\n- 正常路径: `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick && go test ./internal/cmd/... ./internal/handler/... -timeout 60s -v`\n\nSo it builds to `bin/rick`. But the existing convention (task5) builds to a temp dir. Which should I follow?\n\nThe test method is the acceptance criteria, and it explicitly says `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick`. The binary at `bin/rick` is then used for the boundary/exception tests (`./bin/rick plan --dry-run` etc.).\n\nBut building to `bin/rick` overwrites the existing binary in the repo. That's a side effect. However, the test method explicitly says to do this. And `bin/` is probably gitignored (let me check). Actually, `bin/rick` exists in the repo and shows in `git log`? Let me check .gitignore.\n\nActually, the task5 script builds to a temp dir to avoid polluting. But the test method for task6 says `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick`. \n\nI think the safest approach that respects BOTH: build to `bin/rick` as the test method says (it's what the acceptance criteria specifies), OR build to a temp dir and use that binary.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. The test method is explicit: `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick`. And the boundary/exception operations use `./bin/rick ...`. So I should build to `bin/rick`.\n\nBut wait — there's a concern: is `bin/rick` tracked in git? If building modifies a tracked file, it'd show as a dirty diff. Let me check .gitignore and git status.\n\nActually, the important thing is the test works correctly. Building to `bin/rick` is fine as long as it's not causing git issues. Let me check .gitignore.\n\nActually, let me check whether `bin/rick` is tracked."}}
